  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render, and the rule has no actions attached.\n\n**What the data holds:** the sample logs have one document per web request. The fields I used:\n- `timestamp`: when the request arrived.\n- `response`: the HTTP status code. It's a string field, so I used `response.keyword`.\n- `bytes`: response size.\n- `extension`: the file type requested.\n\nThe data also has geo, client IP, host, machine OS, referer and URL fields, which I didn't chart.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-b50ab900 Web logs overview\" has four panels, built on the logs data view:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Responses by status code\n- Average bytes by extension\n\nIt's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-b50ab900-web-logs-overview-cee2ec5721dc\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-b50ab900 5xx spike\" (id `74138dfe-6a94-49f1-be4e-f3faccf5ade8`) checks every minute. It counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` whose `response.keyword` matches `5xx` over the last 5 minutes. It fires when that count is above 10.\n\nThe rule is created disabled by default, so I enabled it to get the every-minute checks.",
